WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.425
Chapter 08 breaks the seven
phases into working tasks.

2
00:00:04.425 --> 00:00:09.023
The durations come from the program's
reference plan for a large fleet with an

3
00:00:09.023 --> 00:00:13.837
existing Configuration Manager estate; a
fleet without one skips most of Phase 2.

4
00:00:13.837 --> 00:00:17.995
Phase 1, three to five weeks:
export and import every GPO,

5
00:00:17.995 --> 00:00:23.814
find the GPO-on-GPO conflicts that OU
precedence hid, classify every setting,

6
00:00:23.814 --> 00:00:30.188
and capture an effective-policy baseline
per device tier with gpresult on a sample.

7
00:00:30.188 --> 00:00:35.436
Phase 2, two to three weeks: enable
co-management for a pilot collection,

8
00:00:35.436 --> 00:00:41.588
set the first workloads to Pilot Intune,
and decide MDM wins over GP per population.

9
00:00:41.588 --> 00:00:44.845
The matching Intune policy
must already exist,

10
00:00:44.845 --> 00:00:49.775
because a co-managed device syncs MDM
policy as soon as a workload moves.

11
00:00:49.775 --> 00:00:54.680
Phase 3, three to four weeks, can overlap
it: build every policy unassigned,

12
00:00:54.680 --> 00:01:00.322
with attack surface reduction and controlled
folder access in audit mode, and peer

13
00:01:00.322 --> 00:01:05.963
review that every setting traces to a
decision record and no setting appears twice.

14
00:01:05.963 --> 00:01:11.272
Phase 4 is the pilot, about five percent
of the fleet, representative across tiers,

15
00:01:11.272 --> 00:01:15.788
regions and departments, with a
seven-day grace period instead of one.

16
00:01:15.788 --> 00:01:19.115
GPOs stay linked: the
pilot measures conflicts by

17
00:01:19.115 --> 00:01:22.687
comparing gpresult with
Intune's per-setting status.

18
00:01:22.687 --> 00:01:27.651
Phase 5 is four waves of three weeks
each, watched for seventy-two hours.

19
00:01:27.651 --> 00:01:31.886
Endpoint Protection goes before
device configuration, because moving

20
00:01:31.886 --> 00:01:36.475
device configuration drags Endpoint
Protection and resource access with it.

21
00:01:36.475 --> 00:01:42.041
Phases 6 and 7 disable the remaining
links, clear tattooed preferences,

22
00:01:42.041 --> 00:01:47.512
then disable each GPO, keep it ninety
days, back it up, archive and delete.

23
00:01:47.512 --> 00:01:52.117
On these estimates the program reaches
Phase 7 in roughly thirty-four weeks,

24
00:01:52.117 --> 00:01:55.113
with the last deletions
about three months later.

25
00:01:55.113 --> 00:01:58.453
Remember that rolling back
a pilot means reassigning a

26
00:01:58.453 --> 00:02:02.060
policy with the old value, not
just removing the assignment.
